    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm still nervous. If we shift the focus to \"directional evidence\" and \"effect size\" to avoid the p-value/N issue, aren't we essentially just glossing over a fundamental statistical weakness? I don't want to look like we're being dishonest about the data's reliability. Is there a way to frame it so the reviewers see the depth of the data without us having to explicitly admit the sample is too small to support the main conclusion?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
